---
const stores = [
  { name: 'On My Mac', detail: 'Any folder on the disk.' },
  { name: 'iCloud Drive', detail: 'A folder macOS keeps in sync across your Macs.' },
  { name: 'Dropbox', detail: 'A folder Dropbox syncs for you.' },
];
---

<section class="section" id="storage">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2><span class="hash">##</span>Your notes are a folder</h2>
    <p>
      There is no database and no account. Each note is a <code>.md</code> file whose
      filename is its title. Open the same folder in Obsidian, in TextEdit, or over SSH
      and everything still works — and if you stop using Milky, there is nothing to export.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every storage option below is the same mechanism, which is why none of them ask you
      to sign in. They are all just directories; something else already keeps them in sync.
    </p>

    <dl class="stores">
      {stores.map(({ name, detail }) => (
        <div class="store">
          <dt>{name}</dt>
          <dd>{detail}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
      <div class="store">
        <dt>Git repository</dt>
        <dd>
          A folder that happens to be a clone. Syncing commits your edits, rebases on the
          remote, and pushes — using your own <code>git</code>, your own keys, and your own
          remote. Milky never sees any of it.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .stores { margin: 30px 0 0; max-width: 42em; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .store {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 11.5em 1fr;
    gap: 4px 24px;
    padding: 17px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .store dt { font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.008em; }
  .store dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 15.5px; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .store { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px; }
  }
</style>
